% ============================================================================
% muscle-nuclei-count.query — worked recall queries against
% muscle-nuclei-count.adj (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% Three queries: forward recall with citation, backward recall (bind a
% nuclei value, find the muscle type), and honest abstention on smooth
% muscle.
%
% EACH ROW ANSWERS WITH ITS OWN SENTENCE (RS-5e, #14986), the locator and
% trust inherited from the table envelope. Until that conversion the envelope
% was the skeletal sentence and the cardiac one rode as a `cites`, so a
% cardiac recall was warranted by a sentence about skeletal muscle.
%
% On smooth muscle, narrowly: it is not named in the two sentences
% `tissue-types.adj` quotes in its header, which is where this table's two
% facts were mined. It IS named in that table's shipped `cites`, and the
% cited page does state a nuclei count for it -- so the abstention is about
% this table's chosen scope, not about the page lacking the fact.
% ============================================================================

import "muscle-nuclei-count.adj"

% Forward: how many nuclei does a skeletal muscle fiber have?
? muscle_nuclei_count(skeletal, $Nuclei)

% Backward: which muscle type has a single nucleus per cell?
? muscle_nuclei_count($Muscle, single_nucleus)

% Honest abstention: smooth muscle is outside this table's scope (see above).
? muscle_nuclei_count(smooth, $NucleiSmooth)
